-- title:
--   Finite chart data covering places where sᵢ has least order
-- statement:
--   Fix $N\ge 1$ and work in the field $F=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ of the intermediate field of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated by the divisor expansions attached to level $N$. Let $s:\mathrm{Fin}\,r\to F$ satisfy `IsEmbBasis N s`, i.e. the $s_l$ are linearly independent over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and span the Riemann–Roch space of the divisor `embDivisor N` $=\;$`embDegree N`$\cdot\overline\infty$, and fix an index $i$. Then there are an $m\in\mathbb N$ and families $z_a\in F$, $u_{a,k}\in F$ and $G_{a,k}\in\overline{\mathbb Q}[X][Y]$ indexed by $a<m$ and $k<r+1$, together with integer data $cz$, $cu$, $M$, such that: $z_a=\sum_l cz_{a,l}\,(s_l s_i^{-1})$ and $u_{a,k}=\sum_l cu_{a,k,l}\,(s_l s_i^{-1})$ (integers acting through $\overline{\mathbb Q}$); conversely $s_l s_i^{-1}=\sum_k M_{a,l,k}\,u_{a,k}$ for every $a,l$; each $G_{a,k}$ is irreducible; all coefficients of all $G_{a,k}$ are integral over $\mathbb Z$; after mapping the coefficients into $F$, $G_{a,k}(z_a,u_{a,k})=0$ while the $Y$-derivative satisfies $(\partial_Y G_{a,k})(z_a,u_{a,k})\ne 0$ in $F$ (here the inner variable receives $z_a$ and the outer one $u_{a,k}$, and `Polynomial.derivative` differentiates in the outer variable); and finally, for every place $Q$ of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ whose ideals are principal) at which $\mathrm{ord}_Q(s_i)\le \mathrm{ord}_Q(s_j)$ for all $j$, there is an index $a$ with $\mathrm{ord}_Q\bigl(z_a-Q.\mathrm{evalAt}(z_a)\bigr)=1$ and $(\partial_Y G_{a,k})\bigl(Q.\mathrm{evalAt}(z_a),\,Q.\mathrm{evalAt}(u_{a,k})\bigr)\ne 0$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for all $k$, where $Q.\mathrm{evalAt}$ denotes residue evaluation at $Q$ and $\mathrm{ord}_Q$ the normalised additive valuation.
--
--   This packages a finite list of coordinate–uniformiser data on the curve attached to $\Gamma_0(N)$: each datum consists of integral linear combinations of the affine coordinates $s_l/s_i$, an inverse integral relation recovering the $s_l/s_i$, and irreducible plane equations with algebraic-integer coefficients that are separable in the second variable, the data jointly providing a local parameter and nonvanishing derivatives at every place where $s_i$ has least order. It is used by [`ModularCurve.JZero.exists_chart_of_isPivot`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.exists_chart_of_isPivot) to produce non-archimedean discs with uniformly controlled Hensel–Newton radius.
